menu "Wireless Link Configuration"

    config WLINK_CHANNEL
        int "Wi-Fi channel used by the bridge link"
        range 1 13
        default 1
        help
            Both devices of a wireless bridge pair must use the same channel.
            The channel is fixed at start-up instead of following the station
            configuration, so the link also works when no access point is around.

    config WLINK_SWD_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "CMSIS-DAP response timeout (ms)"
        default 250
        help
            How long the wireless host waits for a DAP response from the slave
            before retransmitting the request.

    config WLINK_SWD_RETRIES
        int "CMSIS-DAP request retransmissions"
        range 1 10
        default 3
        help
            Number of times a DAP request is (re)sent before the relay gives up.
            Giving up lets OpenOCD time out and retry at a higher level.

    config WLINK_PEER_WAIT_MS
        int "How long the wireless host waits for the link to pair (ms)"
        default 2000
        help
            A board that has just been reset does not know its peer yet, while the
            host tool sends its first command as soon as the USB device enumerates
            and gives up if that command is not answered. Before sending a DAP
            request the relay waits up to this long for the link to pair, so the
            request can go out as an acknowledged unicast frame.

            After the wait the request is still sent (as a broadcast, which the
            slave receives as well), so this only trades a little latency at
            start-up for reliability.

    config WLINK_SWD_VERBOSE_LOG
        bool "Log every CMSIS-DAP packet on the SWD path at info level"
        default n
        help
            Logs each DAP request, each retransmission, each cache hit and each
            response at info level instead of debug level.

            Useful when diagnosing the wireless SWD path, because the host board's
            console is not always easy to reach. Keep it off for normal use: a debug
            session with a live watch can send hundreds of DAP commands per second,
            and the serial console is far slower than that, so the logging itself
            would slow the link down.

endmenu
